er.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"mdcrd\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3878\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [\"AMBER trajectory format\", \"inpcrd\"], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"AMBER trajectory (also called mdcrd), with 10 coordinates per line and format F8.3 (fixed point notation with field width 8 and 3 decimal places).\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"RST\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3886\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [\"restrt\", \"rst7\"], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"AMBER coordinate/restart file with 6 coordinates per line and decimal format F12.7 (fixed point notation with field width 12 and 7 decimal places)\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"trr\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3910\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"Format of trr files that contain the trajectory of a simulation experiment used by GROMACS.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"Textual file format to store trajectory information for a 3D structure .\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [\"CG trajectory formats\", \"MD trajectory formats\", \"NA trajectory formats\", \"Protein trajectory formats\"], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"File format to store trajectory information for a 3D structure .\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"Topology format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3879\"}, \"children\": [{\"text\": \"GROMACS top\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3880\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"GROMACS MD package top textual files define an entire structure system topology, either directly, or by including itp files.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"AMBER top\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3881\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [\"AMBER Parm\", \"AMBER Parm7\", \"Parm7\", \"Prmtop\", \"Prmtop7\"], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"AMBER Prmtop file (version 7) is a structure topology text file divided in several sections designed to be parsed easily using simple Fortran code. Each section contains particular topology information, such as atom name, charge, mass, angles, dihedrals, etc.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"PSF\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3882\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"X-Plor Protein Structure Files (PSF) are structure topology files used by NAMD and CHARMM molecular simulations programs. PSF files contain six main sections of interest: atoms, bonds, angles, dihedrals, improper dihedrals (force terms used to maintain planarity) and cross-terms.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"GROMACS itp\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3883\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"GROMACS itp files (include topology) contain structure topology information, and are tipically included in GROMACS topology files (GROMACS top). Itp files are used to define individual (or multiple) components of a topology as a separate file. This is particularly useful if there is a molecule that is used frequently, and also reduces the size of the system topology file, splitting it in different parts.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"CHARMM rtf\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3887\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"Format of CHARMM Residue Topology Files (RTF), which define groups by including the atoms, the properties of the group, and bond and charge information.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [\"CG topology format\", \"MD topology format\", \"NA topology format\", \"Protein topology format\"], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"Format of topology files; containing the static information of a structure molecular system that is needed for a molecular simulation.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"FF parameter format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3884\"}, \"children\": [{\"text\": \"GROMACS itp\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3883\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonyms\": [], \"narrow_synonyms\": [], \"replaced_by\": [], \"consider\": [], \"definition\": \"GROMACS itp files (include topology) contain structure topology information, and are tipically included in GROMACS topology files (GROMACS top). Itp files are used to define individual (or multiple) components of a topology as a separate file. This is particularly useful if there is a molecule that is used frequently, and also reduces the size of the system topology file, splitting it in different parts.\", \"has_input\": [], \"has_output\": [], \"has_topic\": []}, {\"text\": \"AMBER frcmod\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"http://edamontology.org/format_3888\"}, \"children\": [], \"exact_synonym