mat enables publishing structured data as linked data (Linked Data), so that the data can be interlinked and become more useful through semantic queries.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Annotated text format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3780\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"OSCAR format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_1741\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"OSCAR format of annotated chemical text.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"PubAnnotation format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3781\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"JSON format of annotated scientific text used by PubAnnotations and other tools.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"BioC\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3782\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"BioC is a standardised XML format for sharing and integrating text data and annotations.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"PubTator format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3783\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Native textual export format of annotated scientific text from PubTator.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Open Annotation format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3784\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A format of text annotation using the linked-data Open Annotation Data Model, serialised typically in RDF or JSON-LD.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"BioNLP Shared Task format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3785\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"BRAT format\", \"BRAT standoff format\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A family of similar formats of text annotation, used by BRAT and other tools, known as BioNLP Shared Task format (BioNLP 2009 Shared Task on Event Extraction, BioNLP Shared Task 2011, BioNLP Shared Task 2013), BRAT format, BRAT standoff format, and similar.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Data format of an annotated text, e.g. with recognised entities, concepts, and relations.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Query language\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3787\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"Query format\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A query language (format) for structured database queries.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"NMR data format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3824\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"nmrML\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3825\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"nmrML is an MSI supported XML-based open access format for metabolomics NMR raw and processed spectral data. It is accompanies by an nmrCV (controlled vocabulary) to allow ontology-based annotations.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"NMReDATA\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3906\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"MReData is a text based data standard for processed NMR data. It is relying on SDF molecule data and allows to store assignments of NMR peaks to molecule features.  The NMR-extracted data (or \\\"NMReDATA\\\") includes: Chemical shift,scalar coupling, 2D correlation, assignment, etc.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [\"Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy data format\"], \"ns\": [\"NMR peak assignment data\", \"NMR processed data format\", \"NMR raw data format\", \"Processed NMR data format\", \"Raw NMR data format\"], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Data format for raw data from a nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy experiment.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Raw microarray data format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3828\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"GPR\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3829\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"GenePix Results (GPR) text file format developed by Axon Instruments that is used to save GenePix Results data.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [\"Microarray data format\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Data format for raw microarray data.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"NLP format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3841\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"NLP annotation format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3862\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"NLP corpus format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3863\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"NLP format used by a specific type of corpus (collection of texts).\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"An NLP format used for annotated textual documents.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [\"Natural Language Processing format\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Data format used in Natural Language Processing.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"RNA annotation format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3865\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"mirGFF3\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3864\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"miRTop format\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"mirGFF3 is a common format for microRNA data resulting from small-RNA RNA-Seq workflows.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [\"miRNA data format\", \"microRNA data format\"], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A \\\"placeholder\\\" concept for formats of annotated RNA data, including e.g. microRNA and RNA-Seq data.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Trajectory format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3866\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"Trajectory format (binary)\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3867\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"HDF5\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3590\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"Loom\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3913\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"The Loom file format is based on HDF5, a standard for storing large numerical datasets. The Loom format is designed to efficiently hold large omics datasets. Typically, such data takes the form of a large matrix of numbers, along with metadata for the rows and columns.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [\"h5\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"HDF5 is a data model, library, and file format for storing and managing data, based on Hierarchical Data Format (HDF).\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"netCDF\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3650\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"ANDI-MS\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Format used by netCDF software  library for writing and reading chromatography-MS data files.  Also used to store trajectory atom coordinates information, such as the ones obtained by Molecular Dynamics simulations.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"HDF\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3873\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"HDF is the name of a set of file formats and libraries designed to store and organize large amounts of numerical data, originally developed at the National Center for Supercomputing Applications at the University of Illinois.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"PCAzip\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3874\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"PCAZip format is a binary compressed file to store atom coordinates based on Essential Dynamics (ED) and Principal Component Analysis (PCA).\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"XTC\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3875\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Portable binary format for trajectories produced by GROMACS package.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"TNG\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3876\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"Trajectory Next Generation format\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Trajectory Next Generation (TNG) is a format for storage of molecular simulation data. It is designed and implemented by the GROMACS development group, and it is called to be the substitute of the XTC format.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"BinPos\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3885\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"Scripps Research Institute BinPos\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Scripps Research Institute BinPos format is a binary formatted file to store atom coordinates.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Binary file format to store trajectory information for a 3D structure .\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Trajectory format (text)\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3868\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"XYZ\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3877\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"The XYZ chemical file format is widely supported by many programs, although many slightly different XYZ file formats coexist (Tinker XYZ, UniChem XYZ, etc.). Basic information stored for each atom in the system are x, y and z coordinates and atom element/atomic number.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"mdcrd\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3878\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"AMBER trajectory format\", \"inpcrd\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"AMBER trajectory (also called mdcrd), with 10 coordinates per line and format F8.3 (fixed point notation with field width 8 and 3 decimal places).\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"RST\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3886\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"restrt\", \"rst7\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"AMBER coordinate/restart file with 6 coordinates per line and decimal format F12.7 (fixed point notation with field width 12 and 7 decimal places)\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"trr\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3910\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Format of trr files that contain the trajectory of a simulation experiment used by GROMACS.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Textual file format to store trajectory information for a 3D structure .\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [\"CG trajectory formats\", \"MD trajectory formats\", \"NA trajectory formats\", \"Protein trajectory formats\"], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"File format to store trajectory information for a 3D structure .\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Topology format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3879\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"GROMACS top\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3880\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"GROMACS MD package top textual files define an entire structure system topology, either directly, or by including itp files.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"AMBER top\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3881\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"AMBER Parm\", \"AMBER Parm7\", \"Parm7\", \"Prmtop\", \"Prmtop7\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"AMBER Prmtop file (version 7) is a structure topology text file divided in several sections designed to be parsed easily using simple Fortran code. Each section contains particular topology information, such as atom name, charge, mass, angles, dihedrals, etc.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"PSF\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3882\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"X-Plor Protein Structure Files (PSF) are structure topology files used by NAMD and CHARMM molecular simulations programs. PSF files contain six main sections of interest: atoms, bonds, angles, dihedrals, improper dihedrals (force terms used to maintain planarity) and cross-terms.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"GROMACS itp\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3883\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"GROMACS itp files (include topology) contain structure topology information, and are tipically included in GROMACS topology files (GROMACS top). Itp files are used to define individual (or multiple) components of a topology as a separate file. This is particularly useful if there is a molecule that is used frequently, and also reduces the size of the system topology file, splitting it in different parts.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"CHARMM rtf\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3887\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Format of CHARMM Residue Topology Files (RTF), which define groups by including the atoms, the properties of the group, and bond and charge information.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [\"CG topology format\", \"MD topology format\", \"NA topology format\", \"Protein topology format\"], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Format of topology files; containing the static information of a structure molecular system that is needed for a molecular simulation.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"FF parameter format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3884\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"GROMACS itp\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3883\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"GROMACS itp files (include topology) contain structure topology information, and are tipically included in GROMACS topology files (GROMACS top). Itp files are used to define individual (or multiple) components of a topology as a separate file. This is particularly useful if there is a molecule that is used frequently, and also reduces the size of the system topology file, splitting it in different parts.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"AMBER frcmod\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3888\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"AMBER frcmod (Force field Modification) is a file format to store any modification to the standard force field needed for a particular molecule to be properly represented in the simulation.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"AMBER off\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3889\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"AMBER Object File Format\", \"AMBER lib\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"AMBER Object File Format library files (OFF library files) store residue libraries (forcefield residue parameters).\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Format of force field parameter files, which store the set of parameters (charges, masses, radii, bond lengths, bond dihedrals, etc.) that are essential for the proper description and simulation of a molecular system.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [\"Format (typed)\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A placeholder concept for visual navigation by dividing data formats by the content of the data that is represented.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"RDF format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_2376\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"OWL format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_2197\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"OWL Functional Syntax\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3252\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A human-readable encoding for the Web Ontology Language (OWL).\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Manchester OWL Syntax\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3253\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A syntax for writing OWL class expressions.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"OWL/XML\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3262\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [\"OWL\"], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"OWL ontology XML serialisation format.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A serialisation format conforming to the Web Ontology Language (OWL) model.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Turtle\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3255\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"The Terse RDF Triple Language (Turtle) is a human-friendly serialisation format for RDF (Resource Description Framework) graphs.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"N-Triples\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3256\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A plain text serialisation format for RDF (Resource Description Framework) graphs, and a subset of the Turtle (Terse RDF Triple Language) format.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"Notation3\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3257\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"N3\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A shorthand non-XML serialisation of Resource Description Framework model, designed with human-readability in mind.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"RDF/XML\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3261\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"Resource Description Framework (RDF) XML format.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"JSON-LD\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3749\"}, \"ch\": [{\"t\": \"Open Annotation format\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3784\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"A format of text annotation using the linked-data Open Annotation Data Model, serialised typically in RDF or JSON-LD.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}], \"es\": [\"JavaScript Object Notation for Linked Data\"], \"ns\": [], \"rb\": [], \"co\": [], \"d\": \"JSON-LD, or JavaScript Object Notation for Linked Data, is a method of encoding Linked Data using JSON.\", \"hi\": [], \"ho\": [], \"ht\": []}, {\"t\": \"UniProtKB RDF\", \"data\": {\"uri\": \"format_3771\"}, \"ch\": [], \"es\": [\"UniProt RDF\", \"UniProt RDF format\", \"UniProtKB RDF format\"], \"ns\": [\"UniProt RDF/XML\", \"UniP